WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.748
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:05.390
FLINT WATERS: Well, hello there.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.980
I want to thank everybody
for making it to Next 17

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.050
and for braving
through the long day

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:12.980
to come in here
for this session.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.830
This is the closest
in my athletic career

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.570
I've ever come to
batting clean-up,

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.070
to be in here at
the end of the day,

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.420
so thanks so much for making it.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:20.890
I'm Flint Waters.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.000
I was the former CIO for
the State of Wyoming.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.470
I'm now with Google.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.571
It's a long journey
that gets there,

00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:30.070
and I'll talk a
little bit about it.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.790
But we're going to talk quite
a bit over the next hour

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:37.180
about the methods
we went through

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:40.540
and the pain we suffered
and all the things that

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:46.130
came about being the very
first state to go Google.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:52.670
And to kind of capitalize on
the sentiment behind the need

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.370
to drive quality of
life improvements

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.140
for the citizens
of Wyoming, I want

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.730
to step back to
the very beginning

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:04.760
of my journey with
Google as a partner,

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:10.580
and it started, oh, 2002, 2003.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.310
At the time, I was the lead
of the Wyoming Internet Crimes

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.480
Against Children task force,
and the United States Attorney

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.960
for the District of
Wyoming was Matt Mead.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.020
And what I did at that time
is I spent a lot of time

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.080
on the internet looking for
predators, folks that targeted

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.990
children, and when
I very first started

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:34.110
doing those types
of operations, I

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.050
noticed that I had the
potential, through technology,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:42.540
to very dramatically impact
the lives of our citizens.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.240
And after the first couple
of undercover arrests--

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.670
I used to pose as a little
girl on the internet.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.170
I'm going to let you shake
that image off for a moment.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.440
But after the first
couple of arrests,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.080
I found quite a few
indicators of guys

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.334
that were physically
abusing little bitty kids,

00:01:58.334 --> 00:02:00.000
and I started doing
some research on how

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.890
we could find them, because
the big trend at that time

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.560
was to hang out in chat rooms
and get these emotionally

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.430
crippled guys that would show
up to meet a 13-year-old girl.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.730
And in the course of working
in those types of cases,

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.680
I found guys that were
targeting infants.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.040
They were targeting toddlers,
and I wanted to catch them.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.800
And so in conjunction with
the US Attorney's Office,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.390
I developed some
algorithms that would

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.930
let us find these guys that
were hands-on abusers, that

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.390
were hurting kids,
through the guilt cycle

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.300
that they demonstrated
on the public internet.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.480
And I could identify, based
on their traffic patterns,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.840
the things they were doing
in clear public view,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.540
who was likely a
hands-on abuser of kids.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.210
And I took that to Matt
Mead, the US attorney,

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.890
and I made the
case that I thought

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.530
we could actually
leverage technology

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.400
to rescue those kids.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.370
And the US attorney is
a political appointee,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.392
presidential appointee,
and he had aspirations

00:02:57.392 --> 00:02:58.850
of a political
career, and I didn't

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:01.400
think my odds were
good of convincing him

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.370
that, through these algorithms,
I could successfully

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.300
catch these bad guys.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.950
I keep moving
forward here, sorry.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.582
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:03:11.582 --> 00:03:13.040
But Matt was always
the kind of guy

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.524
that he measured things based
on is it the right thing to do,

00:03:16.524 --> 00:03:17.690
and so he said let's go out.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:18.273
Let's do this.

00:03:18.273 --> 00:03:20.360
Let's try it out and see
if we can get a rescue.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.900
Our very first search
warrant, we went out.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:22.566
We got a rescue.

00:03:22.566 --> 00:03:24.350
We got a child that
was being abused,

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.990
and we started doing
more and more of these.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:31.400
And what I found was that all
the jurisdictions around me

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.010
were having the same
issues, but there

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.500
was a lot-- they were
very tentative to work

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.660
internet undercover
operations because it

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.000
was difficult, from the
beginning of the investigation,

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.152
to see if they
were in your yard.

00:03:44.152 --> 00:03:46.110
The internet really
challenged law enforcement,

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.050
and it challenged a lot
of the laws early on,

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:51.320
and so we used Google Earth
to give law enforcement

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.630
a graphical representation
of where the offenders were

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.360
in their jurisdiction
and then prioritize

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:00.470
the likelihood of which
ones were hands on abusers.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:03.900
Before long, it was being
used in all 50 states.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.180
We shared this with
all those around us.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.255
We then started sharing
it internationally.

00:04:08.255 --> 00:04:11.870
I taught it at Interpol,
and I taught at Cairo

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.090
and Malta and Australia.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.209
And it ended up
going global, and we

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:19.760
rescued thousands
and thousands of kids

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.760
on the back of that technology.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:28.040
And over the course of that, it
really punctuated the sentence

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.310
that government could have
a strong impact on quality

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.520
of life by appropriately
engaging technology.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.350
And so I was always
favorable to Google

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:39.569
because the way they allowed
me to leverage Google Earth--

00:04:39.569 --> 00:04:40.610
we didn't have any money.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.770
We had no funding for
this initiative-- allowed

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.960
me to go out and make
those things happen.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.970
Well, the US
Attorney Matt Mead--

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:50.060
I retired from law
enforcement 2008.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.980
When Matt Mead ran
and then became

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.760
governor of the
State of Wyoming,

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.750
he asked me to come into
the state as the CIO,

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.860
and I got the opportunity
to serve under the mission

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:04.100
that he wanted us to figure out
how to do for all of government

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.470
what we had done for kids.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:10.910
So the tasking was empower the
business needer, the needs,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.370
to be able to innovate
in the way that you did.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.800
That was the challenge he
gave me walking in the door.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:21.100
The prior CIO,
Bob von Wolffradt,

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.380
had just signed the contract
to be the first state

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.650
to go Google, so they were
starting the early adopters

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.710
the week that I came in as CIO.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.510
And I want to talk a
little bit about it

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.120
because it was interesting,
the day I walked in.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:34.870
I walk in.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.615
I walk into the administrative
assistant for the CIO,

00:05:37.615 --> 00:05:39.490
and I say, OK, here's
what I'm going to need.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:40.750
I need a MacBook.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:41.740
I need an iPhone.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:42.580
I'm going to need--

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.200
she said, no, you
can't have that.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.730
I said, well, why not?

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:50.904
Well, this is-- we use
Windows 7 and a BlackBerry.

00:05:50.904 --> 00:05:52.320
And I said, OK,
well, I understand

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.260
that, except I need a
MacBook, and I want an iPhone.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.125
And I want-- she said, no,
that hasn't been approved,

00:05:58.125 --> 00:06:00.000
and I said, all right,
who has to approve it?

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:00.990
She said the CIO.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:07.144
I said this is me.

00:06:07.144 --> 00:06:09.060
You could hear the hush
fall over the building

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.520
because I was one of
those that used a Mac,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.020
and in government, that's
not-- that's startling.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:16.920
And I think a lot of
it was because there

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.990
was such a strong vested
interest in the expertise we

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.730
already had.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.236
There was this
commitment and belief

00:06:26.236 --> 00:06:28.110
in the investment in
ourselves for the things

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.740
we knew how to run,
which is sometimes

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.250
contrary to this
concept of innovation,

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.430
and that's what led me
to the doorstep of what

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.290
Wyoming took on moving forward.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.920
Now, here's my first key for
you that I would recommend.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.380
If you're a state, and you're
looking to move to the cloud,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.254
you're looking to
innovate, I would recommend

00:06:48.254 --> 00:06:52.170
that you get what I had, which
is a very tech-aware governor,

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:55.800
someone heavily committed to
tech as a quality of life issue

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.840
because it makes a huge
difference to helping

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.010
us get started.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.440
But I want to speak to
this, but it's not going

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.120
to be about where we've been.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:03.620
That's it.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.020
That was the history, as
why I reached that point,

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.260
and it got me to the point where
we had all of these rescues,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.769
so if I completely
blew this, they

00:07:12.769 --> 00:07:14.060
would go, oh, that's all right.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:15.030
Get out of here.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.530
At least you did something
good in your life.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:17.040
I'd be OK.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.960
I was able to take a
little bit of risk.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.090
But it was far more about
where we were going,

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:26.430
and I say this because I saw
RFPs coming across my desk that

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.690
were formatted about
wanting new technology,

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.480
and included in the RFP was
all the limitation tying it

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.310
to where we had been.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.140
It was all the language
that restricted it

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.990
to how we had
always done things,

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:43.110
and I was struck
by the fact that we

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.020
want you to help us
innovate, but we really

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.520
don't want to change anything.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.860
That leads you to a
rather difficult decision,

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.960
and it really empowers a
CIO to make a bold shift.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.850
And that's what we
tried to take on.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.280
One thing that I learned
early on in this,

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.380
there's two things
that people hate--

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.830
change and the way things are.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.650
So I was positioned in a point
that, no matter what I did,

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:11.700
we were going to struggle
with perceptions and outcomes.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.130
And so many of the folks
that I was working with,

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.550
that were advising me,
again, were heavily

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.850
invested in the status
quo, and they did not

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:25.527
recognize that the status
quo has to be defended.

00:08:25.527 --> 00:08:27.610
If you wish to believe
it's the way to keep going,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:29.754
you need to demonstrate
why, and they soon

00:08:29.754 --> 00:08:31.170
learned that the
worst excuse they

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:34.200
could give coming into my
office was because we've always

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.510
done it that way,
because in tech we've

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.289
come to understand that the
way we've always done it,

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:46.240
by design, is wrong,
because we're moving.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.890
We're shifting.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:51.640
Even to the point that,
in many of our agencies,

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:55.750
every challenge was
met by a server.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:58.000
We want you to work on
sex offender registry.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.100
I must buy a server.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.690
And this was not
a virtual machine.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:05.320
This was sticking in a rack.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.550
Now, early on, these
were not even in racks.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:11.380
These were desktop units
set in hallways of agencies.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:15.170
We were completely
decentralized,

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.964
so almost every agency
had their own IT team.

00:09:18.964 --> 00:09:20.630
They had their own
stacks they built to.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:22.880
They might be in a data
center, or their data center

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:26.520
might be a closet,
might be a hallway.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:27.524
It was fascinating.

00:09:27.524 --> 00:09:29.690
We had one of our departments,
Department of Health,

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.380
had their own
little data center.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.710
And the first time
I went into it,

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.320
there was sheets of plastic
hanging from the roof,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.544
taped together, because
the roof leaked,

00:09:39.544 --> 00:09:41.960
and they wanted to route the
water away from the computers

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:43.940
because they're--

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:45.342
that's what we were--

00:09:45.342 --> 00:09:47.366
I say this because we
were so far behind.

00:09:47.366 --> 00:09:48.740
There's a couple
of folks in here

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.060
that are hiding their head.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.850
I'm not going to point you out.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.710
It was an interesting
point for us to take on,

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:56.510
but it was also an
excellent opportunity

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:01.640
to engage for a big win
for the State of Wyoming.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:03.800
As we moved to Google,
we made it a point--

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:05.799
and this is something
that Google doesn't really

00:10:05.799 --> 00:10:09.810
speak about a lot, that I think
is a message that's lacking.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:15.300
And that is it is this
massive and amazing ecosystem.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.730
It's not just G Suite.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.350
It's not just cloud.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:21.950
The fact is there's so much
there that is interconnected,

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:25.700
that is capable of expansion
and leveraging components

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:26.780
to benefit.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.690
I don't believe anyone else
has this same combination,

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.180
because when you look
at it, from Android,

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.940
we've got all the
mobile capabilities.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.910
We've got the full
productivity suite.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.430
We've got unlimited storage.

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:42.530
We've got massive
database engines

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.250
we can tie in to App Engine,
containers, all the things

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:48.110
that we can build against.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:49.580
It was a gift.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:51.110
For me, as a new
CIO, it was a gift.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:54.050
Now, I get it, that a CIO--

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.780
especially in government, the
average tenure for a government

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:59.000
state CIO is 28 months.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.220
They're flipping these
guys like hotcakes,

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:04.629
which means you're really
up there taking a bullet.

00:11:04.629 --> 00:11:06.920
I felt, a lot of the time I
would get up in the morning

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.919
before I went to work,
concerned that today

00:11:08.919 --> 00:11:11.210
might be the day that we had
a major breach on a system

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.630
I haven't been able to
get to the cloud yet,

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.917
and I was going to step in front
of a round for the governor.

00:11:16.917 --> 00:11:19.250
There was a lot of what we
did, but as we moved through,

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:21.800
we got some really
amazing changes

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.150
that we didn't anticipate,
and I didn't plan very well.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:27.907
Now, this session is
designed so that you

00:11:27.907 --> 00:11:29.990
can come up and ask
questions, and here's a chance

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:31.970
to get me on recording.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:34.490
And you can ask the how,
you can ask the why,

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.624
what we ran into as we were
building these things out,

00:11:37.624 --> 00:11:39.290
and I will be extremely
candid with you.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.930
The only thing I won't
do is cast aspersion

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:43.190
on another vendor.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:44.790
That's not Google's way.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.850
It was my way six months
ago, but it's not today.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:52.640
We really started capturing
and propagating ideas

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:54.410
at the speed of
thought, and I say

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:58.190
that because it's really a big
deal to a lot of workflows.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:03.620
Google was born in the cloud, so
they've known that from birth.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.440
Now, states are a
little different,

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.880
and a lot of companies are
a little bit different.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:10.760
If you hold that the Lord built
the heavens and earth in six

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.520
days, you have to accept he
had no legacy data to weigh him

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.020
down.

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:17.651
We had a lot to try
and pick-- it's OK

00:12:17.651 --> 00:12:18.650
if you pretend to laugh.

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.290
I pretend to be funny.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:23.780
There's a lot of challenge
there in bringing up

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.810
all these archaic systems that
the states are so heavily tied

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:29.080
to.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:32.780
We went through and
replaced 15 email systems,

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.040
different directories.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.290
We reduced our email
admins from 13 to two.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:42.410
Now, we serve an enterprise
of 10,000 executive branch

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:48.100
employees, and we also deployed
solutions for Wyoming schools.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.140
So we put out 100,000 license--

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:53.230
100,000 user license
of Google for Education

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:54.820
and offered it to
any school district

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:56.770
that wanted to go Google.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.620
And we called it Wyo for Life,
and we did this in conjunction

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:01.745
with what we were rolling
out for state government.

00:13:01.745 --> 00:13:06.080
So we were managing
both with two admins.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:08.510
But this empowered us to
completely change the budget

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:11.180
quagmire, to completely
shift how we do business

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:14.300
because, in state
government, the budget prep

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.761
cycle to get your dollars
was about 24 months long.

00:13:18.761 --> 00:13:20.510
And then there was six
months on the front

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.891
of that for your
business case, to get it

00:13:22.891 --> 00:13:24.640
through governance
approval and then start

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:25.639
the legislative process.

00:13:25.639 --> 00:13:28.690
Well, can you imagine
putting a 30-month lead time

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:34.210
on your agile IT
response to a problem?

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:36.100
That's what we faced.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:38.390
We had hundreds of
physical servers.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:42.010
We had two major data
centers on the state campus,

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:45.220
and when I walked in the door,
the president of the senate

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:50.470
was fully prepared to build
us a $40 million data center.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.195
We were going to build
out an IT facility

00:13:52.195 --> 00:13:54.070
very much like the
University of Wyoming did.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:56.170
Wyoming University had
a beautiful data center.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.820
Of course, theirs is a little
different mission than ours.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.520
And I had the
privilege, if you want

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.728
to call it that, of going
in front of the legislature

00:14:02.728 --> 00:14:04.779
and saying, I don't
think it's a good idea.

00:14:04.779 --> 00:14:06.320
Yeah, I'd have a
great corner office.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:07.736
I'd have a beautiful
view, and I'd

00:14:07.736 --> 00:14:10.130
be sitting up here in this
single point of failure.

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:13.730
And it's not what I felt Wyoming
needed for the long game.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:18.780
So instead we took $16
million, and we built out

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:23.760
100-S gigabit IPv6 backbone
throughout the entire state.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.930
Instead of building our own data
center, we moved to the cloud,

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:29.790
and we built up backbone.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.220
So now, there's towns
in Sheridan, Wyoming,

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:36.420
little bitty beautiful little
town, 7000, 8,000 people.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.010
I should know this.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:39.580
I used to live there.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.970
There's two vendors you
can get a 100-gig internet

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:45.180
from in that little
town because instead

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.160
of investing in
our own facility,

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.770
we invested in a
backbone that reinforced

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.080
the broadband providers
throughout Wyoming

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:53.680
and lifted everyone.

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:57.420
We went from dead last for
broadband capacity for schools,

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:58.440
for education--

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.270
we were like five
kilobits per second

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:05.610
per student, peak utilization--
to first, all the way to first.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:08.760
And the way we did that was by
getting out of the data center

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.080
business.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.550
I mean, how arrogant
of me to think

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.800
that I could manage the
cooling and the power

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.890
and the physical plant and the
security for our physical data

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:19.520
centers better
than Google could?

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:21.620
And so I didn't
even bother trying.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.330
And you ask the
question, what prompts

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:24.413
you to make that decision?

00:15:24.413 --> 00:15:27.330
Well, I asked them to
bring me the certifications

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:30.050
of our physical data centers.

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.500
I have those here.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:33.610
There was nothing.

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:37.000
We didn't go through and
have those certifications.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:38.650
We were not--

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:40.490
I wouldn't say we
weren't compliant.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:42.760
But we weren't
externally validated.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:46.270
We did have audits on certain
types of data content,

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:49.930
but we were still running
everything physical.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.300
One of the other
big opportunities

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:58.300
that we picked up from
disrupting that quagmire was we

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:01.360
moved from buying physical
servers and that budget cycle

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.970
and Capex to Opex.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:05.830
Because as we
moved to the cloud,

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:08.470
everything shifted to a
contracted negotiation

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:10.540
with Google or our
other partners.

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:13.810
Now, Google wasn't the only
cloud partner that I built on.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:15.430
It wasn't the only
one that I engaged.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:20.500
It made sense for me to
go out and try and do

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:24.689
a diverse approach,
diversify my cloud portfolio.

00:16:24.689 --> 00:16:26.230
Remember what I said
at the beginning

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:29.200
about I will not speak
ill of anyone else?

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.950
So I won't tell you
how that project went.

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:33.450
I'll just tell you with Google,
it went really, really well.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.010
A couple other things that
we picked up early on,

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.770
we dramatically reduced
our VPN deployments

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:42.713
because we pushed
so much offsite.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:51.350
This was quite a lift
because prior to this,

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:53.810
almost every state employee
had a VPN connection.

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:56.540
They could get into
the campus from home.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.459
They were able to beachhead
malware on their home computer

00:16:59.459 --> 00:17:01.250
and then deliver it
directly into our core.

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:03.110
It was a phenomenal design.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:05.060
Didn't work as well
as we had thought.

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:06.930
We were able to get
out of that business.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:10.880
We replaced buying servers
and started buying services.

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:14.220
And we were able to
close our data centers.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.420
Now, that's unheard of.

00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:20.440
We closed our
physical data centers.

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:23.550
And I would love to
claim that this was

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:26.550
brilliant foresight on my part.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:27.720
It was not.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:30.150
It made sense to prepare
ourselves for cloud.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:31.290
And I was all in.

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:34.900
I had moved our agency
entirely into Google.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:38.490
We had no drive shares,
had no drive letters.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:41.040
We had no SharePoint instances.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.040
Everything was sitting on
Google's cloud for our file

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:47.080
services, which meant I could
function anytime, anywhere,

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:48.480
from any device.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:49.480
And then, this happened.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.270
So I don't have a pointer here.

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:58.330
But just above and to
the right of the crane

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:02.050
there, see the large hole in
the side of that building?

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.480
That's my primary data center.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:07.970
The legislature
decided that they

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:10.790
were going to approve the
remodel of the capitol

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:13.790
and the adjoining building,
and I had six months

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:18.221
once the funding was released to
vacate my primary data center.

00:18:18.221 --> 00:18:19.220
Now, this is government.

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:22.910
We take six months to talk
about what we might do.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:24.230
It was a pretty big move.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:29.780
Now, by then, Wyoming IT folks
had become such an amazing--

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:31.550
well, they were
an amazing group--

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:35.990
but they had shifted to an
off-premise dependency, which

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.380
made it really amazing
how quickly they

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:42.440
were able to make this
push, and shift and vacate

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:43.430
that data center.

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:44.516
And they did it.

00:18:44.516 --> 00:18:45.390
And it was very cool.

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:46.431
Now, this was my primary.

00:18:46.431 --> 00:18:50.140
This was my good data center
that we closed out of.

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:52.790
And I want to speak a little
bit about that, because I didn't

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:59.030
know quite how brilliant the
IT staff were that I inherited

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:01.010
as I came into the state.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:02.810
Because I was always
under the impression

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:05.840
that they were pretty
heavily risk-averse.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:07.400
And as I started
looking into it,

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:11.040
they had very good
reason to be risk-averse.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:17.630
They had not really been
free to innovate and break.

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:21.920
The concept of fail early
wasn't really introduced.

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:23.510
And so I had some
difficulties getting

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:26.134
them to take those chances,
to take a leap of faith.

00:19:26.134 --> 00:19:28.550
So what I started doing is
every meeting that I would walk

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:31.610
into with the staff, at the
beginning of the meeting,

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:34.700
I would write a one and
a big asterisk behind it.

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:37.420
It's from my law
enforcement days.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:38.920
And then, I explained
to them, look,

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:40.520
I'm asking you to take a chance.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:42.080
I'm asking you to invent.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:43.710
I'm asking you to take risk.

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:45.170
And at the end of
the day, there's

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:46.460
only one ass to risk here.

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:47.900
And it's mine.

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.370
If it blows up, it's on me.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:53.130
You take the chance,
and I will back you.

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.520
Once they tested that a little
bit and a little bit more,

00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:00.450
I soon became amazed at
how they could invent.

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:03.541
They just hadn't been turned
loose to do that before.

00:20:03.541 --> 00:20:05.040
They really did
some amazing things.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:08.280
Now, I mentioned before this
was my primary data center.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:11.170
I also had a secondary
backup data center.

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.620
But the backup data
center was a little old.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.564
We were in an old
junior high school.

00:20:15.564 --> 00:20:17.480
The IT offices were an
old junior high school.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.830
And the data center
was in the old gym,

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:24.120
which had a wood floor, which
is an interesting choice

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:28.260
for a data center, wood
being not as necessarily

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:30.810
safe for where we were
building out our equipment.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:31.650
That was my backup.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.800
And the generator
that ran it, we

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:37.350
were on an EPA
waiver that allowed

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:39.150
us to turn it on to test it.

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:41.280
It was so out of date
we couldn't start it

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:42.750
until we got the waiver.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.280
So we knew it was
going to go away.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:47.670
And we were preparing
to have it replaced.

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:49.540
I tell you that because
of this picture.

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:52.860
I don't know if you know
this, I'm not an electric guy.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:55.170
I do know that they explained
to me that generators

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:58.080
have a certain amount of
magic smoke inside them

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.930
that makes them able to deliver
the power we need if we lose

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:02.715
connectivity to the city.

00:21:02.715 --> 00:21:03.840
Well, that's our generator.

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:07.550
And that's all the magic
smoke escaping out of it.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:10.800
It burned up, seized
up the diesel.

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.130
I mean, seized tight.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:14.570
This was right in
the middle of us

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:18.350
moving out of the
primary data center.

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.440
Now, normally, if you are
dependent on two data centers,

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:24.380
this is going to be a pretty
impactful moment in your CIO

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:25.580
career.

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:28.610
We were really, really fortunate
because we had built our help

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:31.490
ticket system in App Engine.

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:33.950
So we had already moved
it completely off premise.

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:36.410
We had built out
all of our core IT

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:39.380
onto the Google side for
the enterprise agency.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:43.610
So we didn't actually lose any
service time as this went out.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:46.580
As we moved forward, we
finally got to the point

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:49.156
where we no longer had
on-premise data centers.

00:21:49.156 --> 00:21:50.780
The only thing left
there, now, there's

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.430
a mainframe for the
Department of Transportation.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:54.290
I put glass in it.

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:55.623
And I make money on the tickets.

00:21:55.623 --> 00:21:57.284
I bring people in and show them.

00:21:57.284 --> 00:21:58.700
And I'm not picking
on mainframe--

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:00.449
it's great technology--
but all the people

00:22:00.449 --> 00:22:03.100
that run it are gone.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:04.850
It was really, I had
a guy hidden in Idaho

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:06.975
in the mountains that was
there to help me bail out

00:22:06.975 --> 00:22:08.252
if we had any issues.

00:22:08.252 --> 00:22:09.710
That sounds like--
it's not a joke.

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:13.369
I actually had a
guy hidden in Idaho.

00:22:13.369 --> 00:22:15.410
If Tony Young is here,
he's not going to be happy

00:22:15.410 --> 00:22:16.100
that I gave that up.

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:16.910
Tony, are you out there?

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:17.409
No?

00:22:17.409 --> 00:22:19.471
OK.

00:22:19.471 --> 00:22:19.970
All right.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:23.810
What Google did for us, it
really became a catalyst

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:25.670
for cultural change.

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:28.970
And we reinvented
how we delivered IT.

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:31.520
But we had to learn a couple
of pretty tough lessons

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.410
to make this succeed.

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:37.590
We moved to a more agile
business management approach,

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:39.780
and we changed the
employee empowerment

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:40.800
to invest back in folks.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.220
And this is one of my
change management ideas

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:46.740
to point out for you.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.380
Many of the folks
that were around me

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:54.600
were quite concerned that if we
went towards a no-ops mindset,

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.760
I wasn't going to
need them anymore.

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.740
This was job threatening.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.190
Now, I can accept that it
was expertise threatening

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:05.820
because it required that
they learn new skill sets,

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.500
or expand on their skill sets.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.810
But there's a belief
a lot of times

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:11.790
when you start this
engagement that it is also

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:13.620
going to be job threatening.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:16.530
And it took a lot of effort
to engage those folks

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:18.150
and win them over.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:22.260
We started a very aggressive
employee training program.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:25.890
We started pushing folks to
learn cybersecurity roles.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.770
We started pushing to
expand on their skill set.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:31.590
By the time we got
fully into this process,

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:34.770
our folks ended up with far
more impressive resumes.

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:37.230
They were far more adept
at doing what we did.

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:40.950
And we finally got out of
having folks that ran cables.

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:44.420
We got out of folks that
had to crawl under rocks

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:47.200
and troubleshoot equipment.

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:48.820
We really minimized
how that was,

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:50.740
but it's not an easy shift.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:54.190
And one of the things that I
would say in engaging that,

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:59.220
find your malcontents
and engage them.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:01.890
And I loved my malcontents.

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:06.600
Because folks who are not
happy with how things are,

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:08.550
if given the right
tools, are the folks

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:12.600
that can make some of the
most amazing improvements.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:16.530
George Bernard Shaw talked about
how the reasonable man complies

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:19.200
to fit his world and
the unreasonable man

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:22.330
demands that his world
change to fit him.

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:26.070
I totally butchered the
quote, but so all change,

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:29.287
all progress is based
on unreasonable men.

00:24:29.287 --> 00:24:31.120
Well, there's something
to be said for that.

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.872
I found the folks that did
not like how things were

00:24:33.872 --> 00:24:35.830
and empowered and equipped
them to try and help

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:36.820
make that change.

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:37.870
And it was a pretty
big difference.

00:24:37.870 --> 00:24:39.411
Because those are
the same folks that

00:24:39.411 --> 00:24:42.040
can find amazing ways to
stop you from doing it,

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:44.170
the same folks that could
find reasons not to.

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:47.170
And I really had to
wrap my head around what

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:51.220
it would take to get
those teams focused

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:52.500
on what we wanted to build.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.820
I'll talk a little bit
about what that entailed.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:57.500
We got rid of our storage--

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.280
our drive storage--
and pushed all of that

00:25:00.280 --> 00:25:03.230
to the cloud behind
filer devices.

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:05.510
We moved all of our
physical data center

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:09.117
off and into a public
data center with the cloud

00:25:09.117 --> 00:25:11.450
on the back end to expand it,
and anything that we could

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:13.760
go straight cloud, we did.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:16.070
This was a pretty massive
shift for government.

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:19.970
But it was also a massive
shift in expectations

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:22.250
for the types of folks we
were bringing in the door.

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:26.000
Because a lot of the teams
that I was bringing in

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.940
were millennials that had
been using this technology

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:29.570
to succeed.

00:25:29.570 --> 00:25:30.694
They were accustomed to it.

00:25:30.694 --> 00:25:32.720
My son-- my son's
in high school--

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:34.310
he comes home from school.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:35.980
He walks into his room.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:36.530
Byooo.

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:38.738
All the lights dim down
while he powers in everything

00:25:38.738 --> 00:25:40.280
that he's got in his backpack.

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:41.090
He jumps online.

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:42.470
He jumps into a Hangout.

00:25:42.470 --> 00:25:44.090
He's with six of his buddies.

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:45.680
And they're doing their work.

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:48.020
They're busting out slide decks.

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.689
They're doing sheets.

00:25:49.689 --> 00:25:51.230
He's in the robotics
club, so they're

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:54.980
designing the next
iteration of their robots.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:56.591
He expects that
type of capability.

00:25:56.591 --> 00:25:59.090
The worst thing I could do to
him, bringing him in the door,

00:25:59.090 --> 00:25:59.756
is handing him--

00:26:02.319 --> 00:26:03.360
AUDIENCE: Something else.

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:03.960
FLINT WATERS: Something else.

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:04.877
Thank you.

00:26:04.877 --> 00:26:06.960
Thank you for-- you could
see where I was looking.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.543
I was just going to
go ahead and jump.

00:26:11.011 --> 00:26:13.260
Giving those folks what they
are accustomed to succeed

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:14.910
was a big win.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:17.910
And it also helped
the expectation

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:19.620
for the delivery of knowledge.

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:22.980
Because we were
accustomed to workflows

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.510
where you would
capture the ideas,

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:27.052
you would capture the principles
in a physical document,

00:26:27.052 --> 00:26:29.010
you would send it out to
seven or eight people,

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.200
you would wait for their
input to come back,

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:32.730
you would reconcile
the differences,

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:34.620
and you would do it again.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:38.700
At best, you were capturing
one frame of mind a day.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:41.580
And we shifted that so that
we would be able to capture it

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:43.424
in real time.

00:26:43.424 --> 00:26:45.090
Probably, you've seen
some of the demos,

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:46.830
or if you're accustomed
to the tools,

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:48.120
you've seen the folks
jumping and doing

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:49.860
all the simultaneous authoring
and all the pieces that

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:50.910
came together.

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:53.730
Very powerful shift.

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:55.470
And Google is
accustomed to that.

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:56.640
We were not.

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:58.620
We had to reinvent how we did.

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:00.120
We actually tackled workflows.

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.400
I tried to convince
folks to look

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:06.750
at every workflow they engaged
and disassemble it and reinvent

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:07.410
it.

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:09.630
And we'll talk a
little bit more.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:10.650
This was my office.

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:14.670
Originally, I had this
big battleship of a desk.

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:20.299
And it had an $18,000 compressed
digital video unit on it

00:27:20.299 --> 00:27:21.840
that hadn't been
used in a long time.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:24.240
It was like a Yugo
sitting there.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:26.302
And folks would come in
and sit in front of it.

00:27:26.302 --> 00:27:27.510
And I got rid of all of that.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:29.190
And we worked on the round.

00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:31.920
And we took GVCs--

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:33.600
Chromebox for Meetings--

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:37.560
Chromecasts, and we put up
large screens and white boards

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:38.550
everywhere.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:40.770
We closed half of
our meeting rooms

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:42.930
and put these devices
in the hallways.

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:45.240
And we started using Scrums.

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:47.850
We use Scrum, of course,
for Agile development.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.440
But I liked the approach.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.179
So I started using it for
business management, as well.

00:27:52.179 --> 00:27:54.720
We would pull into the meeting
to solve a particular business

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:55.020
issue.

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.030
And somebody would throw
10 minutes on the timer.

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:58.613
And we would work
through the solution

00:27:58.613 --> 00:28:02.280
and build out the answer.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:05.130
It was quite enabling
in giving us--

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:06.037
yes, sir.

00:28:06.037 --> 00:28:07.620
Would you please go
to the mic for me?

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:08.360
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:09.540
FLINT WATERS: And if
anyone has questions,

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:11.430
just step right up because
I'm happy to take them

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:12.390
as we're moving along here.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:14.170
AUDIENCE: I hope you
still have the lightsaber.

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:14.730
That's all.

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:15.646
FLINT WATERS: Oh, yes.

00:28:15.646 --> 00:28:16.350
Well, yeah.

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:17.340
The lightsaber is a standard.

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:18.450
I brought that when
I came to Google.

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:20.220
My team gave me that
as we were changing.

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:25.230
That's basic nerd lore there.

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:26.400
We got rid of--

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.980
we actually got out of
the phone business too.

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:31.770
There was so much that Google
didn't tell me I could do.

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:33.930
And this empowerment
was massive.

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:38.010
A good example, Governor
Mead and his wife

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:42.190
would go to the Middle East
and provide Thanksgiving dinner

00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:44.190
to our guard troops that
were deployed overseas,

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:47.880
the Wyoming guard units,
except that it coincided

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.076
when he needed to do
the annual budget press

00:28:50.076 --> 00:28:52.200
conference, the formal
budget press conference that

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:53.820
had always been done--

00:28:53.820 --> 00:28:55.020
the way they always did it--

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:58.350
in the formal conference room
in the governor's office.

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:00.510
So he went ahead and went.

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.630
We set up a screen in the
formal conference room.

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:06.960
Someone sat in front of him at
an airport in the Middle East

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:11.670
with a Nexus 7, and we did a
Hangout live for his budget

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:12.539
press conference.

00:29:12.539 --> 00:29:13.830
He looked the press in the eye.

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:14.830
He took their questions.

00:29:14.830 --> 00:29:16.320
He joked and laughed with them.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.900
He announced he was
going to run again.

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:21.390
He really leveraged
this technology

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:22.830
as a force multiplier.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:25.950
I saved over a million
dollars a year just

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:29.940
on the dedicated video
equipment that I no longer had

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:35.380
to buy on the backs of what
I got as part of G Suite.

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:40.220
I saved over $500,000
a year on the denial

00:29:40.220 --> 00:29:42.860
of service protection because
I moved the state's website

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.780
to Google Sites.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.660
I moved a lot of what we were
doing up onto the Cloud side.

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:49.560
And it gave us quite
a bit of power.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.470
We streamed the governor's
State of the State address

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:54.300
from inside the
legislative chamber

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:56.370
as a YouTube live event.

00:29:56.370 --> 00:30:00.060
When he did his large
energy proposal and release,

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:02.100
that was done as
a Hangout On Air,

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:04.290
and citizens could
take questions.

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:06.182
They could jump in,
take questions back

00:30:06.182 --> 00:30:08.640
to the governor's office, and
ask about what we were doing.

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:14.310
That was a huge transparency
of government leveraged

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:15.840
by this technology
that we put in

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:18.420
so that we could have
Mail and Calendar.

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:21.180
And this was so much about
where I had made mistakes,

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:24.870
because I did not anticipate
the scale with which this

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:29.220
could change what we did once
I empowered the malcontents.

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:31.320
And please, when I say
that, it's somewhat joking,

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:35.370
but it is endearing for me
because I'm one of those folks.

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:36.120
I'm a disruptor.

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:39.640
I like to break
stuff and reinvent.

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.070
I did have a lot of agencies
that I had to win over.

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:45.442
And so I'm going to share
this little bit with you.

00:30:45.442 --> 00:30:47.150
And you can make fun
of me if you'd like.

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:50.590
This is a bit of a weakness
of mine, but I'm from Wyoming,

00:30:50.590 --> 00:30:53.350
and we have a bucking horse
on our license plates.

00:30:53.350 --> 00:30:56.230
It is a cowboy culture.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:31:01.110
And I had agencies that I had
to engage to convince them

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:03.330
why they should throw out
how they did everything

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:05.220
and reinvent.

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:06.930
And so, this is
what I came up with.

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.720
Sometimes you have
to get off the horse.

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:11.820
And the premise was, if
you're trying to win over

00:31:11.820 --> 00:31:13.700
the brand inspectors,
or the livestock board,

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:15.410
or the Department
of Agriculture,

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:18.660
me explaining the power
of machine learning

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:21.990
and data analytics
isn't necessarily

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:25.360
going to convince them that
this is a worthy journey.

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:27.460
So what I did is I went
in and I met when I said,

00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:31.410
OK, let's assume that it's 1885.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:34.050
And you're moving
your herd from El Paso

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:36.070
to the railhead in Cheyenne.

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:36.570
All right.

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:38.400
Everybody is with me.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:41.140
Now, if it takes you 30
days and the rancher says,

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:43.452
I want you to get
it there in 28 days,

00:31:43.452 --> 00:31:44.910
you can put the
spurs to the horse,

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:47.432
and you can get
there in 28 days.

00:31:47.432 --> 00:31:49.140
But if the rancher
says, I want him there

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:52.730
in three days, what
do you have to do?

00:31:52.730 --> 00:31:55.270
I put it on the screen.

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:56.770
You have to get off the horse.

00:31:56.770 --> 00:31:59.320
You have to reinvent
how you do business.

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:01.330
That's how you explain
moonshot thinking

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:03.140
to Department of Agriculture.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:05.140
I say this premise because
it's really important

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:08.380
to empower the business
leads to help explain how

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:13.360
you justify exponential
increase in capability

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:15.520
versus incremental.

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:17.260
How do you do
exponential improvement?

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:20.950
You have to reinvent
how you go about it.

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:25.120
And with that in mind, we
started running grassroots

00:32:25.120 --> 00:32:27.490
training where anyone in
the state could attend,

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:29.710
any of the state
employees could attend.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:31.480
And I would go in and
just talk about how

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:33.970
to tear down your
current workflow,

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:35.470
your perception of
how business was

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:40.140
done, so that I could get you to
ride Google's innovation curve.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:43.660
Because in the legacy mindset,
we started building solutions

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:46.540
on a physical
server that started

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:50.550
to decay the moment I
put it into production.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:53.660
We had $30 million of
investment into an application

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:58.020
that was tied to a specific
version of the database engine.

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:00.990
And over the 20
years it was expected

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:03.990
to run, halfway through that,
that database engine quit

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:05.232
getting patched.

00:33:05.232 --> 00:33:06.690
It wasn't getting
security updates.

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:09.300
And yet, we still had critical
government information

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:11.460
in there.

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:12.960
So I had to convince
them that it

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:16.080
was time to start
building on the top

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:21.644
of an upwardly mobile framework
and give them that flexibility

00:33:21.644 --> 00:33:23.060
that even if we
didn't get funding

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:25.940
to enhance it next
year, App Engine

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:30.020
was continually enhanced,
made even more secure,

00:33:30.020 --> 00:33:31.910
optimized by Google.

00:33:31.910 --> 00:33:33.661
So our investment
continued to rise

00:33:33.661 --> 00:33:35.410
in functionality and
capability because it

00:33:35.410 --> 00:33:36.590
was riding that curve.

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:41.900
I really wanted to
position Wyoming

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:43.640
so that when it was
time to do analytics,

00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:45.056
when we could do
machine learning,

00:33:45.056 --> 00:33:48.590
our data was secure, positioned
well, and we could operate.

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:50.660
I did not realize when I
did it that we were also

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:54.560
going to free up
enough budget that we

00:33:54.560 --> 00:33:56.900
could pay for the innovation.

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:58.390
This was the dirty
little secret.

00:33:58.390 --> 00:34:01.400
We freed up far more
than we spent on Google.

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:02.716
Far more.

00:34:02.716 --> 00:34:04.340
I moved systems that
would have cost me

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:08.013
$600,000 to build
on to App Engine,

00:34:08.013 --> 00:34:10.429
and I paid for the monthly
consumption with a credit card.

00:34:15.674 --> 00:34:17.090
The next thing I
would ask of them

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:20.060
is to think about how
much mobile has impacted

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:22.370
your personal life and theirs.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:24.679
If you think right
now, how much different

00:34:24.679 --> 00:34:28.760
is home now because
of those devices?

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:31.699
And would the citizens
feel like the product

00:34:31.699 --> 00:34:36.234
we deliver to them is
equally as impactful?

00:34:36.234 --> 00:34:37.150
And it clearly wasn't.

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:38.530
We weren't keeping up.

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:40.799
We couldn't do it.

00:34:40.799 --> 00:34:42.340
So this was another
capability that I

00:34:42.340 --> 00:34:45.900
had to convince these
folks to rethink, reinvent,

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:46.999
and reengage.

00:34:46.999 --> 00:34:48.790
Now, there are a lot
of areas in government

00:34:48.790 --> 00:34:55.040
where we had clients built to
leverage the software that we

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:58.100
didn't have the funding to
rebuild from the ground up.

00:34:58.100 --> 00:35:00.230
But we wanted to
make it mobile aware.

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:04.010
We wanted to make it
deliverable to citizens.

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:07.310
So we built a
synchronization engine

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:10.970
that would scan a
SQL Server, replicate

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:14.210
the schema in Google's cloud,
and then synchronize the data

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:17.260
one way upwards, which
allowed me to continue

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:19.610
to work on premise--

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:21.680
or in our offsite data center--

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:23.360
against the products
I could afford

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:24.690
that were already in place.

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:28.220
But I could build modern, mobile
apps that could leverage it

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:29.300
for the citizens.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:32.510
So it was kind of a half step
to start modernizing how we

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:34.230
were deploying those solutions.

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:36.860
And that was something that
we, as we moved forward,

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:38.600
found that to be
immensely beneficial.

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:41.270
Because it also pulled all
that citizen activity off

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:45.420
of our network and
carried it upwards.

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:48.570
One of the other pieces that
was a difficult delivery,

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:51.720
and we did finally
get there, is I

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:54.390
needed to get across to folks
that tangible results are

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:58.140
about getting wisdom and
recognition into the mind

00:35:58.140 --> 00:35:59.850
of your target audience.

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:02.130
And I say that because
so much of what we had

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:06.090
was built on the premise
of the printed page.

00:36:06.090 --> 00:36:09.471
The tangible outcome, the
final result, was printed page.

00:36:09.471 --> 00:36:11.970
Now, I won't ask you to raise
your hand, but how many of you

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:15.690
have workflows today
that the first step of it

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:17.739
is to start
formatting a document

00:36:17.739 --> 00:36:20.280
to capture the information that
is going to be in the printed

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:22.110
format?

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.960
I mean, that's just how we've
thought for a long time.

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:26.820
And yet, it's really
not relevant anymore.

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:28.680
Because so much
of this, if I want

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:30.990
to make sure I can get
into the wisdom before you,

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:34.914
I need to make it present
here, not in print.

00:36:34.914 --> 00:36:36.330
When I very first
started and they

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:37.913
got me my first set
of business cards,

00:36:37.913 --> 00:36:40.280
I had a fax number on them.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:44.420
This was 2011.

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:46.864
That was a little sad, actually.

00:36:46.864 --> 00:36:49.640
All right.

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:52.930
Here's my challenge.

00:36:52.930 --> 00:36:54.880
If you are engaging
in this process, one

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:59.560
of the things I would ask
to consider of your folks,

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:02.140
we have traditionally
designed our workflows

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:06.980
based on our perception of the
capabilities of the technology.

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:11.339
Now, that's potentially flawed,
because very few of us--

00:37:11.339 --> 00:37:13.880
now I'm surrounded by a lot of
folks that are brilliant about

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:17.840
this, but in government,
very few of us fully knew all

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:20.630
of the capabilities
of the technology--

00:37:20.630 --> 00:37:23.870
and so we were designing
from a crippled version

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:25.820
of what the potential
outcomes would be.

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:29.840
Rather, I would offer
that you challenge it

00:37:29.840 --> 00:37:31.940
from your perfect
world scenario.

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:34.670
In other words, invent
what your perfect world

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:38.680
would look like without
regard to the technology.

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:45.190
Once that's done, then engage
the tech to rise to your need.

00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:50.860
That shifts from being change
adverse to change expectant.

00:37:50.860 --> 00:37:52.480
It moves it to
where you now want

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:54.340
to see the enhancements
that come out

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:57.310
of an agile, capable
company like Google.

00:37:57.310 --> 00:38:00.040
And this is where
they really shine.

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.490
This allowed us to reinvent
how we started to build.

00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:05.320
And most of the
folks, once you can

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:08.320
engage them to
find this premise,

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:10.960
they can invent a perfect
world that the tech

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:12.456
isn't rising to yet.

00:38:12.456 --> 00:38:14.080
It's getting closer
and getting closer.

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:15.640
But now, as new
updates come along,

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:17.810
they're more
excited to see them.

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:18.520
And that's a win.

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:23.320
That positions us well as you
move from this legacy, build it

00:38:23.320 --> 00:38:27.790
and it's decaying, to
build it on a rising,

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:30.790
continually improving
innovation curve.

00:38:30.790 --> 00:38:33.940
And those changes are
around every corner.

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:35.770
It helps leverage that
expectation of what

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:38.310
they're going to get next.

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:44.460
Now, there are a couple of
things I wish I'd known.

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:48.000
About a year ago, I
realized that we had really

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:51.600
disrupted the IT business
model for the state of Wyoming.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:54.900
And my folks were
doing amazing things.

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:56.850
They were going far
beyond what I dreamed.

00:38:56.850 --> 00:39:00.300
They were doing far
better than I, by myself,

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:01.710
would have figured out.

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:03.770
And I found myself--

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:07.320
my deputy CIO was spending a
lot of time just keeping me

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:11.610
from being out in the hallway
terrorizing the villagers.

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:13.530
And they were going on
and doing great things.

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:15.060
And so, I knew I needed
to move on because I

00:39:15.060 --> 00:39:16.018
was a disruptor, right?

00:39:16.018 --> 00:39:17.250
I like to break things.

00:39:17.250 --> 00:39:18.880
And they were really doing well.

00:39:18.880 --> 00:39:21.180
So I asked the
governor for permission

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:23.880
to pursue a career with Google.

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:26.130
The governor's a great guy.

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:28.260
And we had a lot of
conversation about it.

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:30.570
And he gave me his blessing.

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:33.180
And so, I separated myself
from all business activity

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:34.840
related to Google.

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:39.180
And I started pursuing to
come to Google because I heard

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:43.110
that they were going to be doing
some new, impressive things

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:45.150
that could help
governments do more,

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:50.460
serve more, rescue kids, prevent
fires, change how government

00:39:50.460 --> 00:39:51.330
deploys services.

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.160
And I wanted to be a part of it.

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:54.990
And that's what led
me to go after Google.

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:56.430
Now, I am a Googler.

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:58.539
In September, I left the state.

00:39:58.539 --> 00:39:59.580
And I got on with Google.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:01.980
And now, I'm in the Professional
Services Organization.

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:05.430
So I'm part of the
team that now companies

00:40:05.430 --> 00:40:08.430
can contract to come in and
give them an inside view of what

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:11.040
Google can do, give them
that trusted partner

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:12.450
to help them succeed.

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:13.825
I so wish I'd had that.

00:40:13.825 --> 00:40:15.450
And once I went
through the onboarding,

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:17.100
I learned about Spanner.

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:18.300
Oh, my god.

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:21.240
I would have killed
to have that year ago.

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:23.500
That would have been
a huge game-changer.

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:25.440
I mean, even just Cloud storage.

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:28.020
Had I known that I could
have redirected all my backup

00:40:28.020 --> 00:40:30.810
solutions to cloud storage
and then airline storage,

00:40:30.810 --> 00:40:34.267
I was charging like
$0.34 a gig for backup.

00:40:34.267 --> 00:40:36.350
And I could have pushed
that-- and it was on tape.

00:40:36.350 --> 00:40:38.170
It was on a tape library that
you had to wait four hours

00:40:38.170 --> 00:40:38.994
to load the tapes.

00:40:38.994 --> 00:40:40.910
I could have pushed that
over, you know, what?

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:42.700
7/10 of a penny?

00:40:42.700 --> 00:40:45.224
And it was available to
access in milliseconds.

00:40:45.224 --> 00:40:46.390
I could have bought it back.

00:40:46.390 --> 00:40:47.860
Gosh, I wish I'd
have known that.

00:40:47.860 --> 00:40:50.387
It would have been
very, very cool.

00:40:50.387 --> 00:40:51.220
There's a long list.

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:52.761
And you're probably
seeing them here.

00:40:52.761 --> 00:40:53.990
It is an amazing company.

00:40:53.990 --> 00:40:57.460
And one of the things that
I learned once I got in here

00:40:57.460 --> 00:41:02.620
is how far they go in
measuring their success,

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:06.760
now our success, based on the
success of their customers.

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:10.810
And I was not used to that as
a CIO for the state of Wyoming.

00:41:10.810 --> 00:41:13.700
I had vendors that came in and
told me all kinds of things.

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:16.660
But when things broke, I
wasn't necessarily getting

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:17.664
that same love.

00:41:17.664 --> 00:41:19.330
And I've seen how
those things work now.

00:41:19.330 --> 00:41:20.500
It's quite a little
bit different.

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:21.708
I mean, you look at BigQuery.

00:41:21.708 --> 00:41:24.789
You look at just
moving DR to Nearline

00:41:24.789 --> 00:41:26.080
would have been massive for me.

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:27.330
So many things I wish
I could have done.

00:41:27.330 --> 00:41:28.440
And there have been
a couple of states

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:30.315
that have done some
pretty impressive things,

00:41:30.315 --> 00:41:34.200
some cities have done
really impressive things.

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:36.955
Nicky's going to come in from
SADA and talk for a moment.

00:41:36.955 --> 00:41:38.080
You want to come up for me?

00:41:40.960 --> 00:41:43.670
Nicky is going to
give an example of--

00:41:43.670 --> 00:41:46.470
please, go up to the mic if
you've got a question before we

00:41:46.470 --> 00:41:47.000
transition.

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:49.900
And then, whichever one.

00:41:49.900 --> 00:41:51.360
AUDIENCE: Just
quick, two minutes.

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:55.880
What did you do with
the ERP HCM workloads?

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:58.850
FLINT WATERS: So what's the
percentage of the ERP workloads

00:41:58.850 --> 00:41:59.990
that are failing right now?

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:05.650
If you don't have a really,
really good partner,

00:42:05.650 --> 00:42:07.900
there's an awful
lot of risk there.

00:42:07.900 --> 00:42:09.760
The areas that were
within our control,

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:12.910
we tried to go back and
reinvent and develop solutions

00:42:12.910 --> 00:42:14.740
with partners that
were cloud-aware

00:42:14.740 --> 00:42:16.330
to deploy solutions.

00:42:16.330 --> 00:42:18.100
That's as far as I
can take that answer

00:42:18.100 --> 00:42:20.260
and not talk about
another vendor.

00:42:20.260 --> 00:42:21.576
Is that fair?

00:42:21.576 --> 00:42:23.200
One of the things
that we also did do--

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:24.430
I do want to share this--

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:27.315
as we built out on App Engine
and moved to no-ops deployment

00:42:27.315 --> 00:42:29.440
that leveraged Google's
cloud where I no longer had

00:42:29.440 --> 00:42:31.760
to worry about patching
OSes, I no longer had

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:33.190
to worry about
patching databases,

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:35.295
I could just build
agile solutions,

00:42:35.295 --> 00:42:37.420
all those solutions that
we built in there, we open

00:42:37.420 --> 00:42:39.830
sourced.

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:42.410
Our help ticket system,
our e-procurement system,

00:42:42.410 --> 00:42:44.750
our credentialing system
was all built so that we

00:42:44.750 --> 00:42:46.374
could give it to another state.

00:42:46.374 --> 00:42:47.290
We could hand it over.

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:49.050
Because Wyoming, we don't
compete with Colorado.

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:50.090
It's not like if we
have better numbers,

00:42:50.090 --> 00:42:51.530
we're going to buy them, right?

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.425
We shared everything with them.

00:42:53.425 --> 00:42:54.800
And then, they
can share with us.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:56.216
We can share with
the other states

00:42:56.216 --> 00:42:58.670
that have had the good
vision to move to Google.

00:42:58.670 --> 00:43:01.160
And that continues-- that will
help build that repository

00:43:01.160 --> 00:43:04.962
where we can leverage that cloud
for all of us for the better.

00:43:04.962 --> 00:43:06.920
I'll go and then we'll
do questions afterwards?

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:07.836
NICKY PARSEGHIAN: Yes.

00:43:07.836 --> 00:43:08.420
Sounds good.

00:43:08.420 --> 00:43:09.630
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:10.880
My name is Nicky Parseghian.

00:43:10.880 --> 00:43:12.969
I'm with SADA Systems.

00:43:12.969 --> 00:43:13.510
There you go.

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:14.450
Thank you.

00:43:14.450 --> 00:43:15.770
I'm with SADA Systems.

00:43:15.770 --> 00:43:18.350
We're one of the premier
partners over here with Google.

00:43:18.350 --> 00:43:21.890
We specialize in G Suites,
Google Cloud Platform,

00:43:21.890 --> 00:43:23.600
and also, Google Maps.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:25.100
Today, what I
wanted to talk about

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:32.160
is one of our counties over
here, Douglas County in Omaha,

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:33.750
Nebraska.

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:35.450
I want to talk a
little bit about what

00:43:35.450 --> 00:43:37.790
they did in their use case.

00:43:37.790 --> 00:43:39.380
To give you a little
bit of background

00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:44.750
without going into too much
details, Douglas County,

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:49.050
Nebraska, so Douglas County
Technology Commission, DOTComm,

00:43:49.050 --> 00:43:52.760
they migrated to
G Suites in 2013.

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:55.220
I'm not going to go into
the detail as to why,

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:59.320
or the background, or the RFPs,
which, as Flint mentioned,

00:43:59.320 --> 00:44:01.250
RFPs can be very long.

00:44:01.250 --> 00:44:06.350
What I want to hone in is
more of the afterwards.

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:10.890
Douglas County, about 5,000
employees, 70 departments,

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:15.530
they support the city and the
county, so all the branches,

00:44:15.530 --> 00:44:19.880
ranging from city hall
to the county library

00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:23.570
to the county clerk's
office, all of them,

00:44:23.570 --> 00:44:27.050
70 total, they're
supporting them.

00:44:27.050 --> 00:44:29.120
From a technology
standpoint, when

00:44:29.120 --> 00:44:31.790
we're talking about
empowering people

00:44:31.790 --> 00:44:35.960
and empowering IT, what
we want to come out of

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:39.100
is not a set of things that
we're prescribing to them.

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:41.660
Because, as Flint
mentioned, you're

00:44:41.660 --> 00:44:45.020
used to doing
things your own way.

00:44:45.020 --> 00:44:48.380
So if we are talking
about RFPs, we've

00:44:48.380 --> 00:44:50.160
been doing emails like that.

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:52.700
And this is exactly,
I'm taking things here,

00:44:52.700 --> 00:44:55.340
I'm putting you over there,
and I'm done with the day.

00:44:55.340 --> 00:44:57.500
When you're presented
with new challenges,

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:02.120
traditionally you're
looking at a board,

00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:03.650
and you're checking boxes.

00:45:03.650 --> 00:45:05.450
Does that solution has this?

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:06.830
Does he have that?

00:45:06.830 --> 00:45:08.420
You're looking to
find and purchase

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:12.920
solutions that are going to
be feeding your use case.

00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:14.810
You don't really look--

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:17.060
I do that every
day, to be honest.

00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:20.150
I think I'm a big offender
amongst the entire room

00:45:20.150 --> 00:45:20.960
over here.

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:24.930
But usually we don't look
at what we have right now,

00:45:24.930 --> 00:45:27.050
which is a big mistake.

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:29.900
With that said,
however, Douglas County

00:45:29.900 --> 00:45:33.080
over here, what
happened in early 2016

00:45:33.080 --> 00:45:36.650
is that the ACLU of
Nebraska essentially

00:45:36.650 --> 00:45:39.410
published a research
that really instilled

00:45:39.410 --> 00:45:42.590
some changes across the
state and the counties

00:45:42.590 --> 00:45:43.880
within the state.

00:45:43.880 --> 00:45:45.920
Without going into details,
what they found out

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:52.220
was if you deprive
children of human contact

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:54.759
and interactions, so in
the corrections office,

00:45:54.759 --> 00:45:56.550
in the corrections
department, essentially,

00:45:56.550 --> 00:45:59.570
if somebody is
misbehaving, you're

00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:03.260
going to put them in isolation,
just like with the adults.

00:46:03.260 --> 00:46:06.470
But if you deprive
them of human contact

00:46:06.470 --> 00:46:09.270
for more than four
hours consecutively,

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:12.560
it turns out that it
has some dire effects

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:16.430
both on the physical side
and on the psychological side

00:46:16.430 --> 00:46:17.900
for those children.

00:46:17.900 --> 00:46:21.620
So the state wanted to make a
change right away, of course,

00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:26.120
and make sure that all the
counties and all the correction

00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:29.630
officers, correction locations,
were able to provide the best

00:46:29.630 --> 00:46:32.180
care for the children.

00:46:32.180 --> 00:46:36.230
Douglas County volunteered
to be one of the pioneers

00:46:36.230 --> 00:46:38.720
to build that
platform and report.

00:46:41.450 --> 00:46:44.930
As you can imagine, the
state is given a mandate

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:47.420
and you're ending
up in a room where

00:46:47.420 --> 00:46:49.610
you have a lot of people
trying to figure out

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:50.810
how do we do things?

00:46:50.810 --> 00:46:51.590
How do we track?

00:46:51.590 --> 00:46:53.630
And how do we make sure
that those children are

00:46:53.630 --> 00:46:55.790
being reached every four hours?

00:46:55.790 --> 00:46:59.720
That's the business requirement.

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:02.450
What they did was
very innovative.

00:47:02.450 --> 00:47:04.400
They looked at what we have.

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:06.080
They didn't have budget.

00:47:06.080 --> 00:47:08.600
That thing happened
in January of 2016.

00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:09.680
Budgets are closed.

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:10.662
There's nothing.

00:47:10.662 --> 00:47:11.870
There's no funding available.

00:47:11.870 --> 00:47:14.010
You can't buy a
solution anymore.

00:47:14.010 --> 00:47:16.820
So they looked at what they had.

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:18.710
And they went to the whiteboard.

00:47:18.710 --> 00:47:20.870
They have a lovely
conference room over there.

00:47:20.870 --> 00:47:22.400
I've been there
quite a few times.

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:24.500
They just whiteboarded
the business process.

00:47:24.500 --> 00:47:26.960
They made it very
simple, which is,

00:47:26.960 --> 00:47:29.450
I'm going over there whenever
somebody is in isolation.

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:31.556
I got to go and check
on a few things,

00:47:31.556 --> 00:47:33.680
make sure that first, they
are all right, and also,

00:47:33.680 --> 00:47:37.817
can they go back into the common
area with the other children?

00:47:37.817 --> 00:47:39.400
To do so, you have
a few requirements.

00:47:39.400 --> 00:47:41.930
Of course, you have a few
boxes that you have to tick.

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:43.420
And that's what they did.

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:47.410
They built very simple processes
with tools that they had.

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:49.300
Everybody has mobile devices.

00:47:49.300 --> 00:47:53.500
They went, themselves, on G
Suite with our help in 2013.

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:55.480
So we knew, we all
knew, that they

00:47:55.480 --> 00:47:59.350
had Gmail and mobile phones.

00:47:59.350 --> 00:48:02.210
As a result, what we
did was we leveraged

00:48:02.210 --> 00:48:05.390
Google Drive, Google
Forms as an input.

00:48:05.390 --> 00:48:07.174
We didn't have to
design any interfaces.

00:48:07.174 --> 00:48:08.590
We didn't have to
design anything.

00:48:08.590 --> 00:48:11.710
All we had to do is,
what are the questions?

00:48:11.710 --> 00:48:13.120
Focus on the content.

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:14.560
Don't focus on the form.

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:17.740
To Flint's point,
many people focus

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:22.600
on the interfaces and the design
before focusing on the content.

00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:23.710
Forget about all that.

00:48:23.710 --> 00:48:27.040
Trust that Google is going to
give you a platform for that.

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:30.430
So they built forms,
and all that information

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:34.210
fed into a few sheets,
into a few Google Sheets.

00:48:34.210 --> 00:48:35.710
And what they had
to do from there

00:48:35.710 --> 00:48:38.260
is literally just
monitor that information

00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:41.080
and build some metrics,
some graphs, some reports,

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:47.630
to present that to the county,
and to the state, sorry.

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:53.690
With our help, they were
able to do from stage zero

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:57.210
to 100% in just a few months.

00:48:57.210 --> 00:48:58.250
It was very impressive.

00:48:58.250 --> 00:49:01.460
And in fact, they were at
like 95% when we went there.

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:05.030
We literally helped them getting
the most out of the platform

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:10.880
by leveraging Google Sheets
and Google Apps Scripts.

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:12.110
Sorry.

00:49:12.110 --> 00:49:17.180
What we did is we've
broken down the process

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:20.810
to business requirements,
kept it simple,

00:49:20.810 --> 00:49:22.450
kept it straightforward.

00:49:22.450 --> 00:49:25.850
What do you need to do
to accomplish your task?

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:29.540
And then, transform out of it.

00:49:29.540 --> 00:49:32.900
With that said, I
think this example

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:36.500
is a very good transformational
example of an organization that

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:40.400
essentially uses the tools
that are at their disposal.

00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:44.600
I urge you, and I invite
you, to look at the Suites.

00:49:44.600 --> 00:49:48.770
What you have right now,
many of you, just like us,

00:49:48.770 --> 00:49:51.650
moved to G Suites
primarily for email.

00:49:51.650 --> 00:49:53.690
At first, you didn't want
to manage your emails.

00:49:53.690 --> 00:49:58.400
You didn't want to have a server
somewhere, no matter which

00:49:58.400 --> 00:49:59.840
brand or manufacturer.

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:02.030
You just wanted
to get rid of it.

00:50:02.030 --> 00:50:03.890
So G Suite was your
point of contact.

00:50:03.890 --> 00:50:07.620
And with it, you gained a lot of
tools, a lot of functionality,

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:09.740
a lot of beef.

00:50:09.740 --> 00:50:13.900
Whether your users adopted it
or was not really your concern.

00:50:13.900 --> 00:50:16.300
Because you had
your ROI already.

00:50:16.300 --> 00:50:19.250
Nowadays, though, if you're
switching and looking

00:50:19.250 --> 00:50:21.422
at the overall value of
the Google Platform--

00:50:21.422 --> 00:50:23.630
and I hope that you're going
to learn a lot of things

00:50:23.630 --> 00:50:25.340
throughout those three days--

00:50:25.340 --> 00:50:29.150
you can have a lot of very
interesting optimization

00:50:29.150 --> 00:50:33.320
that you can do, whether you're
using G Suites over here,

00:50:33.320 --> 00:50:39.210
or Flint mentioned App Engine,
Compute Engine, Dataflow,

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:40.370
BigQuery, I think.

00:50:40.370 --> 00:50:41.720
You have a lot of these.

00:50:41.720 --> 00:50:43.490
I can't even name all of them.

00:50:43.490 --> 00:50:48.650
There's literally, I think,
60 or 70 of these services.

00:50:48.650 --> 00:50:53.720
Just focus on what you need,
use exactly what you want,

00:50:53.720 --> 00:50:55.160
and then iterate out of it.

00:50:55.160 --> 00:51:00.180
Constantly innovate and iterate
so that you can stay on top.

00:51:00.180 --> 00:51:00.680
Right?

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:03.710
Google is going to
iterate on their product.

00:51:03.710 --> 00:51:06.950
I was sitting in a conversation,
in a session this morning,

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:09.740
Google Sheets, in
2006, apparently

00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:14.740
could not handle more
than 1,000 cells of data.

00:51:14.740 --> 00:51:18.410
So imagine back then if you had
to build this workflow on this,

00:51:18.410 --> 00:51:20.990
it would have faced
quite a few limitations.

00:51:20.990 --> 00:51:25.310
But sure enough, without
changing a lot of things,

00:51:25.310 --> 00:51:27.380
Google just innovated
the back end

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:30.200
and made it a wonderful
product as it is today.

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:33.710
Trust that the platform is going
to get even better and better

00:51:33.710 --> 00:51:37.160
without having to have any
negative impact, upgrade,

00:51:37.160 --> 00:51:38.900
platform, or anything like that.

00:51:38.900 --> 00:51:41.670
And just build your
workflows, day in, day out.

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:44.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

